  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Reversal Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert the Fourier series coefficients of $x(t)$ to those of $x(-t)$ by replacing each harmonic coefficient with the coefficient at the opposite index and interpreting the spectra as harmonic-index reversals.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Reversal Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Reversal Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the complex Fourier series, time reversal is a harmonic-index reversal. The coefficient at harmonic $k$ in $x(-t)$ comes from the coefficient at harmonic $-k$ in $x(t)$, so the coefficient sequence is read backward around $k=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} C_k e^{jk\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by substituting $-t$ into the series. This changes the sign in each exponential, but it does not change the coefficient values themselves.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(-t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} C_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now reindex the sum so the exponent returns to the standard positive-$k$ form. That reindexing gives $C_k^{x(-t)} = C_{-k}^{x(t)}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(-t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} C_{-k} e^{jk\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $k=0$ coefficient stays fixed, and each matched pair of harmonics $k$ and $-k$ swaps sides. So the magnitude and phase spectra of $x(-t)$ are mirror images of those of $x(t)$ across harmonic index $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Reversal Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Mapping One Fourier Series Coefficient Under Time Reversal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given a coefficient table for $x(t)$ with entries around $k=0$, such as $C_{-2}=1+j$, $C_{-1}=2$, $C_0=3$, $C_1=2-j$, and $C_2=1-j$, determine $C_2$ for $x(-t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a coefficient table centered at $k=0$. For time reversal, the coefficient at harmonic $k$ in $x(-t)$ comes from the coefficient at harmonic $-k$ in $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_2^{x(-t)} = C_{-2}^{x(t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the given table, the opposite-index coefficient is $C_{-2}=1+j$. Keep that value unchanged and assign it to the target index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_2^{x(-t)} = 1+j",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reversed signal uses the same coefficient value at the mirrored harmonic index: the lookup changes from $-2$ to $2$, but the coefficient itself does not change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Reversal Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Reversing a Full Fourier Coefficient Sequence",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the coefficient list for $x(t)$ with the five harmonics $k=-2,-1,0,1,2$, write the full coefficient list for $x(-t)$ in the same index convention.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a finite coefficient list centered at $k=0$. Time reversal does not change the coefficient values; it reverses the harmonic order around $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_{-2}^{x(t)}, C_{-1}^{x(t)}, C_0^{x(t)}, C_1^{x(t)}, C_2^{x(t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the original list from most negative to most positive. Then move each coefficient to the opposite harmonic index: $-2$ swaps with $2$, $-1$ swaps with $1$, and $0$ stays fixed.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_{-2}^{x(-t)} = C_2^{x(t)}, C_{-1}^{x(-t)} = C_1^{x(t)}, C_0^{x(-t)} = C_0^{x(t)}, C_1^{x(-t)} = C_{-1}^{x(t)}, C_2^{x(-t)} = C_{-2}^{x(t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the full coefficient list for $x(-t)$ is the same list read backward around $0$. The center term stays in place, and the left and right sides swap positions.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "C_{-2}^{x(-t)}, C_{-1}^{x(-t)}, C_0^{x(-t)}, C_1^{x(-t)}, C_2^{x(-t)} = C_2^{x(t)}, C_1^{x(t)}, C_0^{x(t)}, C_{-1}^{x(t)}, C_{-2}^{x(t)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Reversal Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Magnitude Spectrum After Time Reversal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the magnitude table $|C_{-2}|=5$, $|C_{-1}|=3$, $|C_0|=2$, $|C_1|=1$, and $|C_2|=4$ for $x(t)$, identify the magnitude table for $x(-t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a magnitude table indexed by harmonic number $k$. For time reversal, the magnitude values do not change; only the index order mirrors about $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|C_{-2}^{x(t)}|=5,\\quad |C_{-1}^{x(t)}|=3,\\quad |C_0^{x(t)}|=2,\\quad |C_1^{x(t)}|=1,\\quad |C_2^{x(t)}|=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Mirror the index order about $0$: $-2$ pairs with $2$, $-1$ pairs with $1$, and $0$ stays fixed. Keep each magnitude value attached to the opposite index instead of recomputing it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|C_{-2}^{x(-t)}|=|C_2^{x(t)}|=4,\\quad |C_{-1}^{x(-t)}|=|C_1^{x(t)}|=1,\\quad |C_0^{x(-t)}|=|C_0^{x(t)}|=2,\\quad |C_1^{x(-t)}|=|C_{-1}^{x(t)}|=3,\\quad |C_2^{x(-t)}|=|C_{-2}^{x(t)}|=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the magnitude spectrum of $x(-t)$ is the left-right mirror of the magnitude spectrum of $x(t)$: the values read $4, 1, 2, 3, 5$ from $k=-2$ to $k=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Reversal Property",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Phase Spectrum After Time Reversal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the phase table $\\angle C_{-2}=-\\pi/2$, $\\angle C_{-1}=0$, $\\angle C_0=\\pi/6$, $\\angle C_1=\\pi/3$, and $\\angle C_2=-\\pi/4$ for $x(t)$, identify the phase table for $x(-t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a phase table indexed by harmonic number $k$. For time reversal, the phase values stay the same while the index order mirrors about $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle C_{-2}^{x(t)}=-\\pi/2,\\quad \\angle C_{-1}^{x(t)}=0,\\quad \\angle C_0^{x(t)}=\\pi/6,\\quad \\angle C_1^{x(t)}=\\pi/3,\\quad \\angle C_2^{x(t)}=-\\pi/4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Mirror the index order about $0$: $-2$ pairs with $2$, $-1$ pairs with $1$, and $0$ stays fixed. Move each phase value to the opposite index instead of changing the phase value itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle C_{-2}^{x(-t)}=\\angle C_2^{x(t)}=-\\pi/4,\\quad \\angle C_{-1}^{x(-t)}=\\angle C_1^{x(t)}=\\pi/3,\\quad \\angle C_0^{x(-t)}=\\angle C_0^{x(t)}=\\pi/6,\\quad \\angle C_1^{x(-t)}=\\angle C_{-1}^{x(t)}=0,\\quad \\angle C_2^{x(-t)}=\\angle C_{-2}^{x(t)}=-\\pi/2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the phase spectrum of $x(-t)$ is the left-right mirror of the phase spectrum of $x(t)$: read from $k=-2$ to $k=2$, the phases are $-\\pi/4$, $\\pi/3$, $\\pi/6$, $0$, and $-\\pi/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
